Refutation complete. Verdict appended to `AUREL_RESEARCH/findings/2026-11-04.md` under a `[REFUTE]` header. No scan processes left running.

**Target.** PROMOTED.md is still empty and cycle1 has no results, so the only candidate was the one claim left inconclusive at 02:40: cycle0 C4, "winning trades dip to −0.5R more often than a random walk predicts". The 02:40 entry set its revival condition as the 2010-2026 scan path test, which the 05:15 run started but never finished because its session ended with the scan in the background. I ran it to completion this time (scan took 1138 s) and added an independent reverse-direction test.

**Verdict: broken.**

| Test | n | Observed | RW null | z | p |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| P(dip ≥0.5R after entry bar \| TP win), pre-registered | 8,367 | 32.8% | 31.9% | +1.85 | 0.064 (α=0.0083) |
| Same, era 2010-20 / 2021-23 / 2024-26 | | +1.7 / −0.1 / −0.7 pt | | | 0.006 / 0.89 / 0.52 |
| P(TP \| dipped to −0.5R), the actionable direction | 12,715 | 21.6% | 22.4% | −2.18 | 0.029 (wrong sign) |
| Same, TIME exits excluded | 12,280 | 22.3% | 22.5% | −0.37 | 0.72 |
| P(dip) overall | 18,775 | 67.7% | 67.9% | | |

- **The entry-bar artifact reproduces over 16 years.** Measured from x=0 the excess looks like z=+5.7, which is what the ledger's z=4.76 was. Measured from the entry-bar close it collapses to z=+1.85.
- **The small residual excess is not stable.** It exists only in 2010-20, vanishes in 2021 onward, and vanishes at a deeper threshold (d=0.7: +0.0 pt). Zero of six era-by-wall subsets pass Bonferroni.
- **A dip carries no predictive content.** After reaching −0.5R, the win rate matches a memoryless random walk exactly. Neither "cut on dip" nor "hold on dip" has any material against the null.

**Self-check on my own lane's script.** The Monte Carlo and placebo nulls in the 05:15 script (25% and 24%) disagree with the closed form (32%). Half of that gap is a weighting bug: pooling across trades weights each by its TP probability, so easy-TP trades dominate. Equal-weighting the closed form reproduces the shift (31.9 → 28.1). Time truncation is not the cause. Observed dip rates match the closed form within 2 points in every TP-distance quartile.

**Most serious remaining counter-evidence, recorded.** The real-data placebo (random same-hour, same-geometry entries) still shows fewer dips before TP (24.5%) than actual wall trades, even equal-weighted. Wall trades may genuinely retest more than random entries. But since P(TP | dip) equals the random walk, that is a difference in path shape, not in win/loss information. Revival would need a hold-and-volatility-matched placebo showing P(TP | dip) above null after Bonferroni.

**Side observations for the research lane**, noted in the entry: the full 2010-2026 scan universe has 19,404 closed re-accel trades with win rate 48.2% and gross mean R of −0.055, so the fitted drift is essentially zero (θ=−0.004 vs the live ledger's 0.22). cycle1's in/out samples use this same universe. The path cache is at `data/refute_c4_scan_paths_2010_2026.json` for reuse.

